```toml meta
uri-path = "ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/carriers"
```

<<~ ahu #/carriers>>

!! A carrier naming no control code

A carrier opens on the sharktooth and the caret, and one carrying no recognised code still
opens a carrier:

<<^>>

<<^ from="lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/carriers">>

!! A lar URI carrying a query and a fragment

<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/handoff?key=value&other=second#/fragment>>

<<~ loulou lar://mara:operator@crossroads/operator.reads.the-record?a=1&b=2>>

!! Strings in each quoting the dialect takes

<<~ ranks name='a single-quoted value' other="a double-quoted value">>

<<~ ranks title=[[a bracketed value]] other='second'>>

<<~ ranks bare=unquoted spaced = "value after spaces">>

The colon spelling names a parameter too, and it takes a quoted or a bracketed value:

<<~ kue voice:Mischief-Muse key:"a floor recites the bearings instead of inhabiting them">>

<<~ hoike held:[[a bracketed value carrying spaces]] voice:'a single-quoted value'>>

The equals spelling takes a bracketed value too, whole rather than to its first space:

<<~ sigil bracket=[[a bracketed value]] single='a single quoted value' bare=unquoted>>

!! A stray angle bracket answers the same way it answers in wikitext

A dialect wrapping the wikitext grammar carries its injections or loses what they paint, so a
stray < angle bracket takes the verdict here that it takes there.

!! A lar URI carries a query, and a bearing arrow stands in prose

A query names its terms after the path — lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono?mode=draft&hud=aim — and a
bearing arrow -> reads as a bearing wherever the base grammar names nothing.

!! A lar URI's own query takes a quoted value too

and lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono?form='quoted' carries a named value the way RFC 3986 admits:
a single quote stands among the sub-delims, so it needs no encoding.

A DOUBLE-QUOTED VALUE STANDS OUTSIDE THAT LAW and the grammar reads it anyway —
lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono?form="quoted" spells a character `pchar` never admits, which a
strict reader would want as `?form=%22quoted%22`. The dialect accepts what it meets; the pair
stands here so the reading of both forms gets measured rather than assumed.

A BLOCK CHECK NAMES INFORMATION BY ITS DIGEST, and the base grammar reads a link inside it. Measured,
21 of 22 hashes this house has written carry a CamelCase run the autolink rule takes.

The form repeats as held text, named by its own dialect's fence meta rather than opened a second
time as a live block check:

```memetic-wikitext
<<^ code="&#x0003;">>ni:///sha-256;RLbu7oqpmxUvCAdVD6WWpVjlL6L8xEvHEp8PF32siCg
```

!! An authority with no role still reads as an authority

A roleless authority — lar://scryer@crossroads/the.flat.run — still parts into persona and
place, the same shape a role-ful authority takes, minus the role leaf no colon ever opened.

!! A fence reads as the language its meta names

A `lar://held:inline@nowhere/the.dialect.stays-held` address sits inside a backtick span, held
text rather than an address.

```
lar://held:plain-fence@nowhere/the.dialect.stays-held check ni:///sha-256;held-in-a-plain-fence
```

```toml meta
uri = "lar://held:toml-fence@nowhere/the.dialect.stays-held"
check = "ni:///sha-256;held-in-a-toml-fence"
```

```memetic-wikitext
lar://read:this-fence@nowhere/the.dialect.reads-here check ni:///sha-256;addressed-in-its-own-tongue
```

